module lib/minpack/rosenbrock_figures  documents every variable
  The two figures the reading of lmder's run on Rosenbrock's function puts
  beside its core path: the path of the iterates through the curved valley,
  with the trust region each accepted step was taken in, and the secular
  function lmpar solves on the first pass that needs $\lambda > 0$. They are
  drawn from lmder's hist and from Rosenbrock's residuals; the tests below the
  code hold every number drawn to the gfortran oracle's record of the same run.

rf_eval  @d2a3d96f
  what  Rosenbrock's residuals at x into f, and its Jacobian into J, from
        MINPACK's test function mgh_fcn.
  x     $x$  the point
  f     $f$  the two residuals, on return
  J     $J$  the Jacobian, on return
  data  what mgh_fcn needs to know which problem it evaluates

rf_scaling  @4f3ecdb8
  what  The scaling $D$ that lmder chooses by default: the norm of each column
        of the Jacobian at x, computed with enorm as qrfac computes it.
  x     $x$  the point, the start of the run
  D     $D$  the column norms, on return
  f     $f$  the residuals at x (evaluated along with $J$, not used)
  J     $J$  the Jacobian at x
  col   one column of $J$, for enorm
  j     the column
  i     the row

rf_ellipse  @cff27962
  what  The closed curve $(c_x + a_x \cos t,\ c_y + a_y \sin t)$ into xs and
        ys: an ellipse with semi-axes $a_x$ and $a_y$. A trust region
        $\lVert D p \rVert \le \Delta$ is this ellipse in $x$, with
        $a_j = \Delta / D_j$.
  cx    $c_x$  the center's first coordinate
  cy    $c_y$  the center's second coordinate
  ax    $a_x$  the semi-axis along the first coordinate
  ay    $a_y$  the semi-axis along the second coordinate
  xs    the curve's first coordinates, on return
  ys    the curve's second coordinates, on return
  npts  how many points the curve has
  k     the point being placed
  t     $t$  the angle, from 0 to $2\pi$

rf_level  @461ff515
  what  The level curve $f_1^2 + f_2^2 = c$ of Rosenbrock's function into xs
        and ys. Setting $f_2 = 1 - x_1 = \sqrt{c} \cos t$ and
        $f_1 = 10 (x_2 - x_1^2) = \sqrt{c} \sin t$ makes it one closed curve
        in $t$.
  c     $c$  the sum of squares on the curve
  xs    the curve's $x_1$ values, on return
  ys    the curve's $x_2$ values, on return
  npts  how many points the curve has
  k     the point being placed
  t     $t$  the angle, from 0 to $2\pi$

rf_gauss_newton  @3abcc72c
  what  The Gauss-Newton step from x into p: the solution of $J p = -f$, which
        for a square, invertible $J$ makes the linear model $f + J p$ exactly
        zero.
  x     $x$  the point
  p     $p$  the step, on return
  f     $f$  the residuals at x
  J     $J$  the Jacobian at x

rf_accepted  @69690bcb
  what  The accepted trial steps of a hist: returns how many; start and after
        get the point before and after each, delta the radius it was taken
        in.
  why   hist records each trial with the point after it; the point a step
        started from is the point after the previous accepted one.
  hist   lmder's record, one row per trial step
  rows   how many rows hold trial steps
  x0     $x_0$  the starting point
  start  the point each accepted step started from, one row each, on return
  after  the point each accepted step reached, on return
  delta  $\Delta$  the radius lmpar was given for each accepted step, on return
  nacc   how many accepted steps have been found
  cur    the point the next step starts from
  j      a coordinate
  r      a row of hist

rf_path_figure  @c1566055
  what  Draw the first figure: Rosenbrock's level curves and valley floor,
        the accepted iterates of lmder from x0, the trust region of each
        accepted step as the ellipse it is in $x$, and the rejected first
        trial with a dashed line from the start. Returns the number of
        accepted steps.
  hist     lmder's record, one row per trial step
  rows     how many rows hold trial steps
  x0       $x_0$  the starting point
  D        $D$  the scaling: the first Jacobian's column norms, which is $D$ on
           every pass of this run (a test checks it)
  start    the point each accepted step started from
  after    the point each accepted step reached
  delta    $\Delta$  the radius of each accepted step
  nacc     how many steps were accepted
  cx       the first coordinates of the curve being drawn
  cy       the second coordinates of the curve being drawn
  k        a level, an accepted step, or a point of the path
  floor_x  $x_1$ values across the window, for the valley floor
  px       the path's first coordinates: the start, then each accepted point
  py       the path's second coordinates
  p        $p$  the Gauss-Newton step from the start: the first trial

  collect   the scaling, and the accepted steps with the point each started from and its radius
  levels    Rosenbrock's level curves, and the valley floor $x_2 = x_1^2$
  regions   the trust region of each accepted step, around the point it started from
  path      the accepted iterates, joined
  rejected  the first trial, the Gauss-Newton step, marked where it landed

rf_damped_step  @c2b43201
  what  The step u of the damped normal equations
        $(J^\top J + \lambda D^\top D)\, u = J^\top f$ at $\lambda$ = lam, and
        its scaled length $\lVert D u \rVert$, which it returns.
  why   lmpar solves the same system from the QR factorization, without
        forming $J^\top J$. On this small, well-conditioned problem the normal
        equations are accurate enough, and solving it the other way checks
        lmpar independently.
  J    $J$  the Jacobian
  f    $f$  the residuals
  D    $D$  the scaling
  lam  $\lambda$  the damping
  u    $u$  the step with its sign flipped, $u = -p$, on return
  N    $J^\top J + \lambda D^\top D$  the damped normal matrix
  j    a diagonal entry of N

rf_q2  @311cb2ef
  what  $\lVert q \rVert^2 = v^\top (J^\top J + \lambda D^\top D)^{-1} v$ with
        $v = D^\top D u / \lVert D u \rVert$: the number lmpar divides by in its
        Newton step for $\lambda$.
  how   lmpar gets it by solving with its triangle, $q = S^{-\top} P^\top v$;
        since $S^\top S = P^\top (J^\top J + \lambda D^\top D) P$, the two agree.
  J    $J$  the Jacobian
  D    $D$  the scaling
  lam  $\lambda$  the damping
  u    $u$  the step at lam
  N    $J^\top J + \lambda D^\top D$  the damped normal matrix
  j    a diagonal entry of N
  v    $v$  the scaled direction $D^\top D u / \lVert D u \rVert$
  w    $(J^\top J + \lambda D^\top D)^{-1} v$  the damped system solved for v

rf_secular  @579d5e07
  what  lmpar's search for $\lambda$ on a step that is too long, done again from
        its formulas with the normal equations: returns how many values of
        $\lambda$ it tries, with each value in lams, $\phi$ there in phis, and
        the first bracket $[\lambda_\ell, \lambda_u]$ in bracket.
  how   The same rules as lmpar: start from the caller's $\lambda$ clamped
        into the bracket; stop when $|\phi| \le \Delta / 10$; otherwise shrink
        the bracket by the sign of $\phi$ and take a Newton step for
        $1 / \lVert D p \rVert = 1 / \Delta$, never below $\lambda_\ell$.
  x        $x$  the point the step starts from
  delta    $\Delta$  the trust radius
  par0     the $\lambda$ lmder hands lmpar
  lams     the values of $\lambda$ tried, on return
  phis     $\phi$ at each, on return
  bracket  the first lower and upper bounds, on return
  f        $f$  the residuals at x
  J        $J$  the Jacobian at x
  D        $D$  the scaling
  u        $u$  the step at the current $\lambda$
  fp       $\phi$  $\lVert D u \rVert - \Delta$ at the current $\lambda$
  parl     $\lambda_\ell$  the lower bound: one Newton step from $\lambda = 0$
  paru     $\lambda_u$  the upper bound, $\lVert D^{-1} J^\top f \rVert / \Delta$
  par      $\lambda$  the value being tried
  ntried   how many values have been tried
  done     whether lmpar's stopping test has passed
  fprev    $\phi$ at the value tried before
  parc     the Newton correction to $\lambda$

  bounds    evaluate $J$, $f$ and $D$ at x; the lower bound from $\phi(0)$, the upper from the gradient; clamp the starting $\lambda$ into them
  repeat until lmpar's stopping test passes
    try       solve for the step at $\lambda$, measure $\phi$, record both, and apply the stopping test
    if the test did not pass
      next      shrink the bracket by the sign of $\phi$ and take the Newton step, never below $\lambda_\ell$

rf_phi_figure  @572fa0de
  what  Draw the second figure: $\phi(\lambda) = \lVert D p(\lambda) \rVert -
        \Delta$ from x on a log axis of $\lambda$, the band
        $|\phi| \le \Delta / 10$ where lmpar stops, the bracket, and the values
        of $\lambda$ lmpar tries from par0. Returns how many it tries.
  x        $x$  the point
  delta    $\Delta$  the trust radius
  par0     the $\lambda$ lmder hands lmpar
  lams     the values of $\lambda$ tried
  phis     $\phi$ at each
  bracket  the first lower and upper bounds
  ntried   how many values lmpar tries
  f        $f$  the residuals at x
  J        $J$  the Jacobian at x
  D        $D$  the scaling
  u        the step at each $\lambda$ of the curve
  lam      the values of $\lambda$ the curve is drawn at
  phi      $\phi$ at each
  k        a point of the curve, or a value tried
  tried_l  the values tried, without the unused space
  tried_p  $\phi$ at each value tried

  curve     $\phi$ at log-spaced values of $\lambda$ up to the upper bound
  marks     the values lmpar tries, and the bracket
